.SH NAME
stty \- set the options for a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBstty\fR [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-g\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Stty\^
sets certain terminal I/O options for the device that is the
current standard input;
without arguments, it reports the settings of certain options.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Reports all of the option settings.
.TP
.B \-g
Reports current settings in a form that can be used
as an argument to another
.B stty\^
command.
.SH MODES
Detailed information about the modes listed in the first five
groups below may be found in
.BR tty (4).
Options in the last group are implemented using options
in the previous groups.
.SS CONTROL MODES
.TP 10m
.BR clocal " (" \-clocal )
assume a line without (with) modem control.
.TP
.BR cread " (" \-cread )
enable (disable) the receiver.
.TP
.BR cstopb " (" \-cstopb )
use two (one) stop bits per character.
(The System 8000 doesn't currently support \fB\-cstopb\fR).
.TP
.B "cs5 cs6 cs7 cs8"
select character size (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.BR hup " (" \-hup )
hang up (do not hang up) DATA-PHONE\*S\*R
connection on last close.
.TP
.BR hupcl " (" \-hupcl )
same as
.BR hup " (" \-hup )
.TP
.BR parenb " (" \-parenb )
enable (disable) parity generation and detection.
.TP
.BR parodd " (" \-parodd )
select odd (even) parity.
.TP
.B 0
hang-up phone line immediately.
\fBNote:\fR this option is provided to hang-up
a line (usually associated with a dial-out modem) and should
not be used on a line associated with a login shell.
.TP
.B "50 75 110 134 150 200 300 600 1200 1800 2400 4800 9600 19200 extb"
Set terminal baud rate to the number given (the speeds supported
by the System 8000 interface).
.SS INPUT MODES
.TP 10m
.BR brkint " (" \-brkint )
signal (do not signal) \s-1INTR\s+1 on break.
.TP
.BR icrnl " (" \-icrnl )
map (do not map) \s-1CR\s+1 to \s-1NL\s+1 on input.
.TP
.BR ignbrk " (" \-ignbrk )
ignore (do not ignore) break on input.
.TP
.BR igncr " (" \-igncr )
ignore (do not ignore) \s-1CR\s+1 on input.
.TP
.BR ignpar " (" \-ignpar )
ignore (do not ignore) parity errors.
.TP
.BR inlcr " (" \-inlcr )
map (do not map) NL to CR on input.
.TP
.BR inpck " (" \-inpck )
enable (disable) input parity checking.
.TP
.BR istrip " (" \-istrip )
strip (do not strip) input characters to seven bits.
.TP
.BR iuclc " (" \-iuclc )
map (do not map) upper-case alphabetics to lower case on input.
.TP
.BR ixany " (" \-ixany )
allow any character (only DC1) to restart output.
.TP
.BR ixoff " (" \-ixoff )
request that the system send (not send) START/STOP characters
when the input queue is nearly empty/full.
.TP
.BR ixon " (" \-ixon )
enable (disable) START/STOP output control.
Output is stopped by sending an ASCII DC3 and started by
sending an ASCII DC1.
.TP
.BR parmrk " (" \-parmrk )
mark (do not mark) parity errors (see
.BR tty (4)).
.SS OUTPUT MODES
.TP 10m
.B "bs0 bs1"
select style of delay for backspaces (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.B "cr0 cr1 cr2 cr3"
select style of delay for carriage returns (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.B "ff0 ff1"
select style of delay for form-feeds (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.B "nl0 nl1"
select style of delay for line-feeds (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.BR ocrnl " (" \-ocrnl )
map (do not map) CR to NL on output.
.TP
.BR ofdel " (" \-ofdel )
fill characters are DELs (NULs).
.TP
.BR ofill " (" \-ofill )
use fill characters (use timing) for delays.
.TP
.BR olcuc " (" \-olcuc )
map (do not map) lower-case alphabetics to upper case on output.
.TP
.BR onlcr " (" \-onlcr )
map (do not map) NL to CR-NL on output.
.TP
.BR onlret " (" \-onlret )
on the terminal NL performs (does not perform) the CR function.
.TP
.BR onocr " (" \-onocr )
do not (do) output CRs at column zero.
.TP
.BR opost " (" \-opost )
post-process output (do not post-process output; ignore
all other output modes).
.TP
.B "tab0 tab1 tab2 tab3"
select style of delay for horizontal tabs (see
.BR tty (4)).
.TP
.B "vt0 vt1"
select style of delay for vertical tabs (see
.BR tty (4)).
.bp
.SS LOCAL MODES
.TP 10m
.BR echo " (" \-echo )
echo back (do not echo back) every character typed.
.TP
.BR echoe " (" \-echoe )
echo (do not echo) ERASE character as
a backspace-space-backspace string.
\fBNote:\fR this mode will erase the ERASEed character on many
CRT terminals;
however, it does
.I not\^
keep track of column position and can be confusing on
escaped characters, tabs, and backspaces.
.TP
.BR echok " (" \-echok )
echo (do not echo) NL after KILL character.
.TP
.BR echonl " (" \-echonl )
echo (do not echo) NL.
.TP
.BR icanon " (" \-icanon )
enable (disable) canonical input (ERASE and KILL processing).
.TP
.BR isig " (" \-isig )
enable (disable) the checking of characters against the special
control characters INTR and QUIT.
.TP
.BR lfkc " (" \-lfkc )
the same as
.BR echok " (" \-echok ).
.TP
.BR noflsh " (" \-noflsh )
disable (enable) flush after INTR or QUIT.
.TP
.BR xcase " (" \-xcase )
canonical (unprocessed) upper/lower-case presentation.
.SS CONTROL ASSIGNMENTS
.TP 10m
.I "control-character c\^"
set
.I control-character\^
to
.IR c ,
where
.I control-character\^
is
.BR erase ", " kill ", " intr ", "
.BR quit ", " eof ", " eol ,
.BR min ", or " time
.RB ( min " and " time
are used with
.BR \-icanon "; see"
.BR tty (4)).
If
.I c\^
is preceded by an (escaped from the shell) caret
.RB ( ^ ),
then the value used is the corresponding CTRL character
(e.g.,
.RB " ^d "
is a
.BR CTRL-d );
.RB " ^? "
is interpreted as <DEL> and
.RB " \- "
is interpreted as undefined.
.TP
.BI line " i\^"
set line discipline to
.I i\^
(0 <
.I i\^
< 127
).
.SS COMBINATION MODES
.TP 10m
.B ek
reset ERASE and KILL characters back to normal
.B ^h
and
.BR ^x .
.TP
.BR evenp " or " parity
enable
.BR parenb " and " cs7 .
.TP
.BR lcase " (" \-lcase )
set (unset)
.BR xcase ", " iuclc ", and " olcuc .
.TP
.BR LCASE " (" \-LCASE )
same as
.BR lcase " (" \-lcase ).
.TP
.BR nl " (" \-nl )
unset (set)
.BR icrnl ", " onlcr .
In addition
.B \-nl
unsets
.BR inlcr ", " igncr ", "
.BR ocrnl ", and " onlret .
.TP
.B oddp
enable
.BR parenb ", " cs7 ", and " parodd .
.TP
.BR \-parity ", " \-evenp ", or " \-oddp
disable
.BR parenb ,
and set
.BR cs8 .
.TP
.BR raw " (" \-raw " or " cooked )
enable (disable) raw input and output (no ERASE, KILL,
INTR, QUIT, EOT, or output post processing).
.TP
.B sane
resets all modes to some reasonable values.
.TP
.BR tabs " (" \-tabs " or " tab3 )
preserve (expand to spaces) tabs when printing.
.TP
.I term\^
set all modes suitable for the
terminal type
.IR term ,
where
.I term\^
is one of
.BR tty33 ", " tty37 ", " vt05 ", "
.BR tn300 ", " ti700 ", or " tek .
.PP
the command
.sp
.RS
\fBstty < /dev/\fIttyx\fR.
.RE
.sp
prints the characteristics of \fIttyx\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
tabs(1),
ioctl(2),
tty(4).
